{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1339,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most things in the universe happen too slowly\nfor us to see them happening. Stars like the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5890,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun take tens of millions of years to form,\nand hundreds of millions of years to orbit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10070,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their galaxies; colliding galaxies take billions\nof years to merge. And yet we have a pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15490,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decent understanding of how all these things\nhappen, because there are so many of them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18980,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the observable universe that we can look\nout and see different versions of similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22360,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "events happening in different places, and\nbecause light takes time to get here we see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26200,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places at different distances at different\ntimes thoughout the history of the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30550,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and from all that we can piece together an\nunderstanding of how stars are born and how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34530,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they die, how galaxies develop and interact,\nand so on. It’s kind of like if you only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38820,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had ten minutes to study how humans grow: you\ncouldn’t see any one person grow very much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43210,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that time, but by looking at humans of\ndifferent ages all around the world, you can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47100,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get a pretty good picture of what a human\nlife looks like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50210,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the very first galaxies to ever form\nwere so small and dim that we don't have nearly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55010,
    "dDurationMs": 4439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as good an idea of how baby galaxies are born\nas we do about how they behave and interact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59449,
    "dDurationMs": 1361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "later in life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60810,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our current understanding is that in the early\nuniverse, before any stars had formed, everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65360,
    "dDurationMs": 10029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was just spread-out gas and a lot of dark\nmatter. Gravity would have caused slightly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75389,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "denser areas of dark matter to attract into\nclumps, pulling in bits of gas until they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79810,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were dense enough on their own to gravitationally\ncollapse and start thermonuclear fusion. A"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84770,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star. Many stars. Clusters of stars and their\nassociated dark matter attracted together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89920,
    "dDurationMs": 4449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and merged, and then those clustered-clusters\nclustered together, eventually forming the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94369,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most distant (and longest-ago) galaxies we\nsee today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97829,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we don’t know exactly how soon after\nthe big bang the clumps of dark matter and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101999,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas formed; or when during the process of\nclumping and clustering the first stars ignited;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106639,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or if there was a minimum dark-matter-clump\nsize necessary to attract enough gas to form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111149,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars; or if the very first star clusters\ncame together to form galaxies at all – they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115340,
    "dDurationMs": 3269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might have been so small and fragile they\nwere blown apart when their own stars went"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118609,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova and the first galaxies may have\nactually formed from a second round of clumping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122539,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gas and dark matter as well as dust from\nthe explosions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125539,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To be honest, we don't even have a good enough\ndefinition of what a galaxy is to know when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129729,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to stop calling something a cluster of stars\nand when to start calling it a galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134319,
    "dDurationMs": 4271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we do know is that today we have bajillions\nof galaxies in our universe, none of which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138590,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "existed 13.8 billion years ago, so somewhere\nin between they must all have been babies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144569,
    "dDurationMs": 5591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big gassy babies surrounded by clumps of dark\nmatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video was made with the support of NASA’s\nJames Webb Space Telescope and the Space Telescope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155120,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Science Institute. The James Webb Space Telescope\nis especially sensitive to the infrared wavelengths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160069,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that light from baby galaxies gets stretched\nto by the time it arrives in our solar system,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164459,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so astronomers are excitedly looking forward\nto using the JWST to learn more about the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168840,
    "dDurationMs": 3509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very first stars and galaxies that ever formed\nin the universe."
    } ]
  } ]
}
